<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Descent of the Ganges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Arjuna's Penance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Descent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>'s Penance, is a huge open-air relief carved on two adjoining granite boulders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> in the Pallava period, probably in the seventh century. Its many figures of gods, sages, people and animals, including life-size elephants, converge on a natural cleft once filled with water. It is part of the Group of Monuments at Mahabalipuram, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/descent-of-the-ganges.jpg" alt="Descent of the Ganges" width="320" height="139" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Descent_of_the_Ganges_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
